= Relationships =

'''A semantic relationship is a specific assertion connecting one knowledge object to another.''' In BITwiki, relationships are used to distinguish implementation from dependency, part–whole composition from derivation, and evidence-based support from mere association.

A link is valuable only when its meaning can be explained. Two pages occurring in the same category, paragraph or research area does not by itself establish a strong semantic relationship.

== The eight defined relationships ==

BITwiki currently defines eight primary page-to-page relationship properties. In each example, the arrow points from the '''source page''' making the assertion to the '''target page'''.

{| class="wikitable"
! Property !! Direction and meaning !! Illustrative assertion
|-
| [[Property:Related to|Related to]] || Material association without claiming a more specific relation || Subject A → Related to → Subject B
|-
| [[Property:Implements|Implements]] || A concrete realization implements a concept, method, specification or protocol || Client software → Implements → Communication protocol
|-
| [[Property:Part of|Part of]] || A component, phase or constituent belongs within a larger whole || Sensor module → Part of → Instrument
|-
| [[Property:Depends on|Depends on]] || The source materially relies on the target for operation, construction or interpretation || Application → Depends on → Software library
|-
| [[Property:Supports|Supports]] || A source object supplies a relevant evidentiary or argumentative basis for another claim || Dataset → Supports → Research finding
|-
| [[Property:Contradicts|Contradicts]] || The source materially conflicts with a target claim, prediction or interpretation || Result A → Contradicts → Hypothesis B
|-
| [[Property:Refines|Refines]] || A later or more specific account improves, narrows or qualifies another while retaining continuity || Revised model → Refines → Earlier model
|-
| [[Property:Derived from|Derived from]] || A claim, artifact or representation materially descends from another || New dataset → Derived from → Original dataset
|}

These examples illustrate how the properties should be used; they are not claims that any named edge is already present in BITwiki's stored graph.

== Stronger claims require stronger justification ==

=== Related to ===

Use this for a meaningful association when the precise relationship is unknown or not yet represented. It should not become an indiscriminate substitute for understanding how two things connect.

=== Implements and Depends on ===

'''Implements''' describes a realization of a design or method; '''Depends on''' describes a material reliance. An application might depend on a library without implementing the library's API specification, and might implement a protocol without depending on one particular vendor implementation. Do not confuse these claims.

=== Part of ===

Part–whole relationships require context: physical components, temporal phases, logical constituents and institutional membership may have different rules. Do not assume every kind of parthood is interchangeable or universally transitive.

=== Supports and Contradicts ===

These are epistemic relations, not automatic truth operators. A supporting article can be weak, methodologically flawed or disputed. A contradiction may concern a restricted claim, measurement or interpretation rather than a whole article.

Record the actual reason for support or conflict through references and prose. See [[BITwiki:Evidence]] and [[BITwiki:Claim validation]].

=== Refines and Derived from ===

'''Refines''' expresses conceptual development while retaining recognizable continuity. '''Derived from''' expresses origin or material lineage; it does not guarantee improvement or correctness.

For example, a revised model may refine an older theory, while a translated text is derived from its source without thereby being theoretically superior. See [[BITwiki:Provenance]].

== Direction, inverse display and persistence ==

An assertion has direction. The stored statement

<pre>
<nowiki>[[Implements::Example protocol]]</nowiki>
</pre>

on the page describing a software implementation denotes an outgoing relation to ''Example protocol''. A reader might want to see the reverse description, ''Implemented by'', on the protocol's page. But that reverse label is not the same as a separately stored inverse assertion.

The repository's <code>bitwiki-runtime-schema.json</code> includes the following '''inverse display labels''':

{| class="wikitable"
! Stored relation !! Inverse label for presentation
|-
| Related to || Related to
|-
| Implements || Implemented by
|-
| Part of || Has part
|-
| Depends on || Dependency of
|-
| Supports || Supported by
|-
| Contradicts || Contradicted by
|-
| Refines || Refined by
|-
| Derived from || Source of
|}

For every entry, the schema sets <code>derive_inverse_assertion</code> to <code>false</code>. These definitions are '''metadata for potential presentation or controlled processing'''; they do not establish that inverse-link rendering or automatic inverse writes have been implemented.

A displayed reverse label, a graph query result, and an additional persisted semantic assertion are three different things. No extra relationship should be written solely because an inverse phrase exists.

== Semantics before inference ==

Some relations are symmetric in a particular logical model; others are directional. Some kinds of parthood permit transitivity; others do not. Causal, temporal and dependency chains may contain contextual qualifications.

BITwiki's eight properties do not, merely by existing, authorize general rules such as:

<pre>
A Depends on B, B Depends on C
therefore A Depends on C
</pre>

The conclusion may be useful for a specific dependency analysis, but only if the relation's semantics and the relevant conditions justify it. The same applies to support, contradiction and refinement: do not treat multi-hop paths as proofs.

A query can traverse recorded edges without asserting that its path is a logically valid deduction.

== What the current implementation checks ==

The eight relationship names and their inverse-display metadata appear in the repository schema and corresponding Lua data file. Their <code>Property:</code> pages declare Semantic MediaWiki type '''Page'''.

The current <code>Module:BITwiki/Compiler</code> validates knowledge-object identity fields—entity type, domain, epistemic status and provenance. It does '''not''' yet implement general relation-domain/range checking, graph inference, contradiction detection or inverse edge generation.

Semantic MediaWiki property assertions can be written in page content and are intended to be durable graph state when the pages are published and parsed. Actual stored values, query behavior and live execution must be checked in the running wiki. Source presence and passing static validation do not certify those facts.

== Quality checks for an edge ==

Before asserting or retaining a consequential relationship:

# Identify both source and target unambiguously.
# Explain why the named relationship—not merely a weak association—holds.
# Confirm direction and the intended level of abstraction.
# Trace factual, causal or evidentiary claims to appropriate support.
# Check whether the relation remains valid after a source, target or version changes.
# Avoid automated inverse assertions or multi-hop deductions without independently justified semantics.
# Where relevant, state domain, time, scope and uncertainty explicitly.

A relationship is a claim made about the world or about documented knowledge; the graph does not certify that claim simply by storing it.

== Extending the relationship vocabulary ==

Relations such as ''Causes'', ''Located in'', ''Authored by'', ''Precedes'', and ''Instance of'' may be important in particular subjects. They are '''not among the eight current primary relationship properties'''. Do not use them as existing semantic properties until separately defined and implemented.

A new relation needs a recurring use case, a clear definition, direction and examples, appropriate types, justified inference behavior if any, query tests, and a migration strategy for older assertions. Avoid automatically promoting domain-specific expressions to universal ontology rules.

